{
  "teamId": 111,
  "year": 2022,
  "narrative": "The 2022 Boston Red Sox were a team that flashed brilliance but could never sustain it long enough to matter. They stumbled out of the gate in April, going 9-13 while their bullpen — particularly Hansel Robles — surrendered crushing home runs at the worst possible moments. The rotation and relief corps combined for a 4.53 ERA that would dog them all year, and the offense, while capable of highlight-reel moments, never developed the consistency a playoff contender requires.\n\nJune was the one month when everything clicked. A remarkable 20-6 record transformed Boston into one of the hottest teams in baseball, powered by unlikely heroes like Franchy Cordero, who delivered a grand slam and a go-ahead three-run shot within two weeks of each other. Rafael Devers was thundering, Alex Verdugo was coming up clutch, and for a brief, shining stretch it felt like the Red Sox had found their identity. The roster was healthy, the lineup was clicking, and even the pitching was holding up.\n\nThen July arrived, and with it, reality. Boston went a dreadful 8-19 that month, a collapse so steep it effectively ended whatever playoff hopes June had kindled. Tanner Houck's inconsistency epitomized the pitching problems, and the offense went quiet just when it needed to roar loudest. August and September produced roughly .500 baseball — competent but meaningless — as the losses piled up and the front office began looking toward the future rather than the standings.\n\nThe 2022 Red Sox finished 78-84, a losing record that reflected a roster caught between eras. Xander Bogaerts would leave for San Diego in the offseason, J.D. Martinez would depart, and the franchise faced hard questions about its direction. The June mirage made the final ledger sting that much more — this was a team that showed it could be great, but only for one month, and that is never enough.",
  "chapters": [
